
  [33mbindForm[39m(formElement, spec, onValid)

  Wires submit handling for one form. On submit it prevents default, reads values with [38;5;153mnew FormData(form)[39m, runs
  [38;5;153mvalidate[39m, and:

  - [1minvalid[0m → renders each message, sets [38;5;153maria-invalid="true"[39m on the offending fields, moves focus to the first one, and
    does [3mnot[0m call [38;5;153monValid[39m.
  - [1mvalid[0m → clears all messages and calls [38;5;153monValid(values, event)[39m.

  [1mMarkup contract[0m — the part every future form must follow, so it's deliberately small:

  1. [1mEach validated input needs a [38;5;153mname[39m attribute[0m matching its spec key. [38;5;153mFormData[39m only sees named fields, and today's
     inputs have [38;5;153mid[39m but no [38;5;153mname[39m — so [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains [38;5;153mname="username"[39m and [38;5;153mname="password"[39m.
  2. [1mError placement is optional.[0m If the form contains [38;5;153m<span data-error-for="username">[39m, the message goes there. If not,
     [38;5;153mbindForm[39m creates one and inserts it after the input. So a new form needs no error markup at all, but can position
     messages itself when the layout demands it. The created element gets [38;5;153mclass="field-error"[39m and is linked to its input
     via [38;5;153maria-describedby[39m.

  [1mFails loudly at bind time:[0m if a spec names a field with no matching input, [38;5;153mbindForm[39m throws an Error naming that field.
  Silent non-validation is the worst outcome here — a typo'd key would mean a field that looks validated and isn't.

  [1mOne UX behavior included:[0m a field's error clears when the user edits it. Without that, corrected fields keep showing
  stale messages until the next submit, which reads as a bug.

